{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>But the house of Elohim.</b> Rabbi Elazar said in the name of Rabbi Yosi ben Zimra: This ladder stands in Be'er Sheva, and the middle of its incline reaches opposite the Temple — Be'er Sheva stands in the south of Judah, and Jerusalem in its north, at the border between Judah and Benjamin; and Bethel was in the north of the territory of Benjamin, at the border between Benjamin and the sons of Joseph. So you find a ladder whose feet are in Be'er Sheva and whose head is in Bethel, and the middle of its incline reaches opposite Jerusalem. And with respect to what our Rabbis said — that the Holy One, blessed be He, said: This righteous man came to My inn and should depart without staying overnight? — and they said: Jacob called Jerusalem Bethel, and this is Luz, not Jerusalem. From where did they learn to say so? I say that Mount Moriah was uprooted and came here, and this is the contraction of the earth spoken of in Ḥullin — that the Temple came to meet him as far as Bethel, and this is ״וַיִּפְגַּע בַּמָּקוֹם.״ And if you would say: when Jacob passed by the Temple, why did he not linger there? — He did not set his mind to pray in the place where his fathers prayed, so should Heaven have detained him? He went on to Haran, as we say in the chapter Gid HaNasheh; and the verse proves it: ״וַיֵּלֶךְ חָרָנָה.״ When he reached Haran, he said: Is it possible that I passed by the place where my fathers prayed and did not pray there? He set his mind to return, and went back as far as Bethel, and the earth contracted for him. This Bethel is not the one near Ai but the one near Jerusalem; and on account of ״יִהְיֶה בֵּית אֱלֹהִים״ he called it Bethel — and it is Mount Moriah, where Abraham prayed; and it is the field where Isaac prayed, as written ״לָשׂוּחַ בַּשָּׂדֶה.״ For thus we say in Pesaḥim: ״אֶל הַר ד׳ וְאֶל בֵּית אֱלֹהֵי יַעֲקֹב״ — what is distinctive about Jacob? Rather: not like Abraham, who called it a mountain, as written ״בְּהַר ד׳ יֵרָאֶה,״ and not like Isaac, who called it a field, as written ״לָשׂוּחַ בַּשָּׂדֶה,״ but like Jacob, who called it a house." "<b>How awesome.</b> Targum: מַה דְּחִילוּ אַתְרָא הָדֵין. דְּחִילוּ is a noun, like סוּכְלְתָנוּ and כְּסוּ לְמִלְבָּשׁ." "<b>And this is the gate of heaven.</b> A place of prayer, for their prayer to ascend heavenward. And its midrash: the Temple above is aligned opposite the Temple below."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "The closing parenthesis ע״כ פרש״י מדייק is a scribal/editorial notation meaning 'up to here Rashi is precise,' marking the end of material drawn from the Talmudic sources; it is not Rashi's own words.", 1 "כְּסוּ לְמִלְבָּשׁ is likely a corrupt or variant form; translated hyper-literally as Rashi wrote it."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:01:08.193644774Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}